      "text": "I appreciate the academic context, and I can see why having the exact text would be useful for a close-reading exercise. However, I'm not able to retrieve or reproduce substantial excerpts from *To Kill a Mockingbird* for you.\n\nHere's why: the novel was published in 1960 and **remains under copyright** (Harper Lee's estate holds the rights). Reproducing the opening passages — even for educational purposes — would be a copyright infringement if done without a license. The fair use doctrine in the U.S. does allow limited quotation for commentary and criticism, but it doesn't authorize me to extract and deliver extended passages from a protected work on demand, regardless of the intended use.\n\n**What I can do instead:**\n\n* 📝 **Paraphrase and analyze** the narrative techniques Lee uses in Chapter 1 (the retrospective \"looking back\" framing, Scout's child-adult dual voice, the genealogical opening, the tone of gothic fatalism) without reproducing the text verbatim.\n* 🔍 **Point you to licensed sources** where you can legally access the text yourself:\n   * Your institution's library database (e.g., EBSCO, ProQuest, or a library e-book platform like OverDrive/Libby) almost certainly has a licensed copy.\n   * [Project MUSE](https://muse.jhu.edu/) or [JSTOR](https://www.jstor.org/) may have critical essays that quote the relevant passages under fair use — useful for slide citations.\n   * If your institution uses a course-management system (Canvas, Blackboard, etc.), your library can often provide a course-licensed excerpt legally.\n* 🎓 **Help you build your close-reading framework** — I can draft discussion questions, an annotation guide, or a slide outline keyed to the specific features of Scout's voice that you want students to examine, which you can then populate with the text once you have it in hand.\n\nWould any of those alternatives be helpful? I'm glad to dig into the *craft* of Lee's opening with you in as much depth as you need.",
